WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.376 --> 00:00:19.280
Hello and welcome to Superstars in History, the talk show where we chat with famous people from the

2
00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.461
past. I'm your host, Faye Moss.

3
00:00:23.841 --> 00:00:29.942
Today's guest was a scientist who pioneered the scientific method with his work on physics.

4
00:00:30.783 --> 00:00:38.689
This superstar also spent a lot of time gazing at the stars, which changed our entire understanding of

5
00:00:38.729 --> 00:00:39.811
the solar system.

6
00:00:40.732 --> 00:00:47.001
Unfortunately, discovering the secrets of the skies got him in a lot of trouble down on Earth!

7
00:00:47.842 --> 00:00:51.187
Please help me welcome Galileo Galilei.

8
00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:08.460
Hello, Faye.

9
00:01:09.741 --> 00:01:11.802
I see you brought a telescope!

10
00:01:12.582 --> 00:01:15.363
I can't wait to hear all about what you saw with it.

11
00:01:16.023 --> 00:01:19.625
But first, tell us a little bit about your early years.

12
00:01:20.605 --> 00:01:24.667
I was born in 1564 in the town of Pisa in Tuscany.

13
00:01:25.507 --> 00:01:27.668
My father was a musician and music theorist.

14
00:01:28.709 --> 00:01:33.150
As a child I played the lute and considered a career in music like my father, but I

15
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:34.891
also thought about becoming a painter.

16
00:01:35.711 --> 00:01:39.593
When I was 11 years old, I was sent to be educated in a monastery.

17
00:01:40.473 --> 00:01:43.515
However, my father had bigger plans for me than becoming a monk.

18
00:01:44.695 --> 00:01:48.557
Oh? Did he want you to become a revolutionary scientist?

19
00:01:49.898 --> 00:01:50.758
Not exactly.

20
00:01:51.318 --> 00:01:54.280
He enrolled me at the University of Pisa to study medicine.

21
00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:59.802
My father's career in music had not provided much income for our family, so he wanted me to

22
00:01:59.842 --> 00:02:01.603
have a well-paying career as a doctor.

23
00:02:02.523 --> 00:02:03.904
I found the studies boring.

24
00:02:04.852 --> 00:02:10.094
In those days learning medicine was just a matter of memorizing and repeating what authority figures said.

25
00:02:10.894 --> 00:02:17.035
But once I began listening to a variety of lectures on campus, I discovered mathematics, which opened a

26
00:02:17.116 --> 00:02:18.036
new world to me!

27
00:02:18.976 --> 00:02:23.837
I begged my father to let me switch from medicine to math, and though he opposed at first,

28
00:02:24.238 --> 00:02:25.238
he eventually agreed.

29
00:02:26.238 --> 00:02:28.319
But you didn't become a mathematician.

30
00:02:29.059 --> 00:02:32.640
You're famous for your scientific observations of nature.

31
00:02:33.588 --> 00:02:37.311
Well, I often said, the book of nature is written in mathematics.

32
00:02:38.192 --> 00:02:40.074
Math is a way to understand the world.

33
00:02:42.276 --> 00:02:45.218
It helped me explain my observations and make predictions.

34
00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:53.585
Today we often think of you as the father of the scientific method, especially with your motion studies.

35
00:02:54.386 --> 00:02:57.129
Can you tell us about your scientific process?

36
00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:03.729
I came up with ideas by carefully observing nature, then I designed experiments to test my ideas.

37
00:03:04.609 --> 00:03:09.430
For example, Aristotle thought heavier objects fall faster than lighter objects.

38
00:03:10.531 --> 00:03:12.651
I had long suspected that he was wrong.

39
00:03:13.691 --> 00:03:18.613
In order to see if objects fall at the same speed, no matter their mass, I dropped things

40
00:03:18.693 --> 00:03:20.933
from high places and noted when they landed.

41
00:03:22.114 --> 00:03:26.835
I also rolled lead balls down inclined planes to study acceleration.

42
00:03:27.795 --> 00:03:30.656
I recorded my observations and used math to explain them.

43
00:03:32.952 --> 00:03:37.734
Speaking of observations, let's talk about that telescope.

44
00:03:38.555 --> 00:03:40.756
Did you invent the first telescope?

45
00:03:42.136 --> 00:03:46.879
No. Someone in the Netherlands had made one first—they called it a spyglass.

46
00:03:47.859 --> 00:03:51.621
It was originally used for spotting ships or seeing far distances on Earth.

47
00:03:52.461 --> 00:03:55.002
Though I had no plans to work from, I built my own.

48
00:03:55.983 --> 00:03:58.164
And I must say that mine was much better!

49
00:03:58.972 --> 00:04:03.494
The first telescopes could magnify things three times larger than what the naked eye could see.

50
00:04:04.454 --> 00:04:08.996
But I was able to improve the magnification at first to eight, and eventually beyond that.

51
00:04:09.836 --> 00:04:14.478
Even though I didn't invent the telescope, I was the first to use one to study the heavens.

52
00:04:15.579 --> 00:04:18.460
Tell us what you saw up there and what it meant.

53
00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:22.361
It was astonishing to be able to see the planets up close.

54
00:04:23.522 --> 00:04:25.042
I saw mountains on the moon!

55
00:04:26.023 --> 00:04:28.544
Most people had thought the moon had a smooth surface.

56
00:04:29.328 --> 00:04:31.709
Oh, and I saw that Jupiter had moons too.

57
00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:34.991
I studied the sun and discovered sunspots.

58
00:04:35.991 --> 00:04:40.293
Most interestingly, I discovered that Venus has phases, just like the moon.

59
00:04:41.173 --> 00:04:46.195
By observing these phases, I was able to disprove the popular idea that Venus orbited Earth.

60
00:04:47.016 --> 00:04:51.117
These observations convinced me that the astronomer Nicolaus Copernicus was right.

61
00:04:52.018 --> 00:04:56.540
Copernicus? He argued that Earth revolved around the sun, right?

62
00:04:57.684 --> 00:05:00.546
Yes. This topic was hotly debated in my day.

63
00:05:01.527 --> 00:05:04.669
Most people agreed with the second-century Greek astronomer Ptolemy.

64
00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:10.153
His system put Earth at the center of the universe, with the sun and other planets orbiting around

65
00:05:10.233 --> 00:05:13.396
it. Even I liked Ptolemy's theory since it was simple.

66
00:05:14.156 --> 00:05:18.920
But after I began observing and studying the planets, it became clear that Copernicus was right.

67
00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:28.071
Your work supporting Copernicus attracted some um, negative attention from the Catholic Church.

68
00:05:29.392 --> 00:05:33.036
The Church, like Ptolemy, believed Earth was the center of the universe.

69
00:05:33.957 --> 00:05:39.682
Church officials strongly thought the Bible supported this idea, and therefore my work was seen as challenging the

70
00:05:39.822 --> 00:05:45.988
Bible! So, for reasons that had more to do with faith than science, the Church tried to silence

71
00:05:46.028 --> 00:05:47.670
me starting in 1610.

72
00:05:48.531 --> 00:05:49.592
Weren't you afraid?

73
00:05:50.536 --> 00:05:55.840
Church leaders had burned other people at the stake for supporting Copernicus' idea!

74
00:05:57.001 --> 00:05:57.821
Indeed they had.

75
00:05:58.742 --> 00:06:01.123
But I had scientific evidence on my side.

76
00:06:01.984 --> 00:06:05.667
As I said at the time, I did not think that the same God who gave us our

77
00:06:05.727 --> 00:06:09.729
senses, reason, and intelligence wanted us to abandon their use.

78
00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:16.154
I believed that science would prevail, and the Church leaders would accept my findings when I explained them.

79
00:06:17.075 --> 00:06:18.876
Alas, I was wrong about that.

80
00:06:19.708 --> 00:06:25.950
In 1633 they arrested you and tried you for heresy, the act of holding a belief different from

81
00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:27.110
the Church's doctrine.

82
00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:33.632
I was put in prison, threatened with torture, and forced to sign a statement recanting my views.

83
00:06:34.692 --> 00:06:38.553
After I did that, I was released from prison but kept under house arrest.

84
00:06:39.493 --> 00:06:42.514
At least I was able to have visitors with whom I could talk about science.

85
00:06:43.334 --> 00:06:47.536
I was also allowed books and even managed to write one, though I had to do that in

86
00:06:47.616 --> 00:06:50.097
secret as I was forbidden to write new books!

87
00:06:50.998 --> 00:06:52.479
House arrest wasn't too bad.

88
00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:54.701
It certainly could have been worse.

89
00:06:55.662 --> 00:06:58.465
Well, I don't want to keep you captive here!

90
00:06:59.426 --> 00:07:01.788
Thank you so much for joining us today.

91
00:07:02.649 --> 00:07:04.791
We'll let you get back to history now.

92
00:07:05.772 --> 00:07:06.352
Thank you, Faye.

93
00:07:06.712 --> 00:07:07.693
I enjoyed talking with you.

94
00:07:10.956 --> 00:07:14.580
Galileo lived out the rest of his life under house arrest.

95
00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:20.224
With the help of his friends, he managed to have his last book published in Holland.

96
00:07:21.025 --> 00:07:25.909
Galileo died in 1642 at the age of 77.

97
00:07:26.789 --> 00:07:35.497
Galileo's confirmation of Copernicus' theory eventually became accepted by his detractors, including the Catholic Church.

98
00:07:36.297 --> 00:07:39.280
He didn't just make an impact on astronomy.

99
00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:47.537
With his innovative experiments and application of math to science, he paved the way for future scientists.

100
00:07:48.517 --> 00:07:54.739
Albert Einstein honored Galileo by calling him the father of modern science.

101
00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:58.861
I hope you enjoyed today's show.

102
00:07:59.241 --> 00:08:01.662
Be sure to tune in again next time!
